# =============================================================================
#  harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol
#  The GnuCOBOL half of the compiled-COBOL ORACLE for the ACAS posting-cycle
#  migration (COBOL -> Python 3.12).
# =============================================================================
#
#  PURPOSE
#  -------
#  This image is the ARBITER. The migration has no pre-existing test suite:
#  compiled COBOL execution IS the behavioural specification, defects included,
#  and a defect reproduced is correct while a defect fixed is a failure. This
#  image is therefore the one place in the project that contains `cobc`, and it
#  exists so that a semantic question about the posting cycle can be settled by
#  RUNNING the maintainer's own programs rather than by reasoning about them.
#
#  It carries two things:
#    1. the maintainer's exact COBOL toolchain -- GnuCOBOL 3.2 final, the MySQL
#       client libraries under /usr/local/mysql, the JC preSQL translator
#       `presql2`, and the bridge's C interface object `cobmysqlapi.o`; and
#    2. CPython 3.12 plus the repository's exactly pinned Python dependencies,
#       so the Python side of every comparison runs on the same host, against
#       the same database, in the same process family.
#
#  Because both cycles are driven from this single image against the single
#  MariaDB served by the sibling `harness/Dockerfile.mariadb`, a non-empty
#  ordering-normalised table diff is always a REAL behavioural difference and
#  never an artefact of the comparison. That property is the whole point.
#
#  POSITION IN THE TREE
#  --------------------
#  `harness/` is the compiled-COBOL oracle tree and is a SIBLING of
#  `acas_posting/`, never a sub-package -- the plan annotates it as "the
#  compiled oracle; NEVER on the package import path". There is no
#  `harness/__init__.py`, this image creates none, and nothing under `harness/`
#  is importable: `pyproject.toml` packages `acas_posting*` only and excludes
#  `harness*`. The harness Python tools (`dump_tables.py`, `normalize.py`,
#  `diff_states.py`) are standalone scripts invoked BY PATH.
#
#  WHY THIS IMAGE ALSO CARRIES CPYTHON -- AND WHY THAT IS NOT AN R-1 VIOLATION
#  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  R-1 ("no COBOL at runtime") constrains the SHIPPED Python package's import
#  path and the shipped artifact -- not a harness container. The plan specifies
#  a TWO-service compose file ("MariaDB service at the version the schema was
#  produced by, plus a GnuCOBOL builder service"), so `run_python_scenario.sh`,
#  `dump_tables.py`, `normalize.py` and `diff_states.py` necessarily execute
#  here as well. The prohibition R-1 protects is preserved structurally and is
#  verifiable rather than promised:
#    * `pyproject.toml` excludes `harness*` from packaging, so the built wheel
#      carries no COBOL, no `cobc` requirement and no `cobmysqlapi.o` linkage;
#    * nothing in this file creates a `harness` package or puts `harness/` on
#      `PYTHONPATH`;
#    * `acas_posting/` contains no subprocess call, no FFI bridge and no
#      linkage to the bridge's C interface object -- the compiled oracle is
#      reached only OUT OF PROCESS, from the scenario and determinism suites.
#  The complementary half of the same separation is enforced in the sibling
#  image: grep `harness/Dockerfile.mariadb` and you will find no COBOL
#  toolchain component of any kind.
#
#  BUILD CONTEXT -- READ THIS BEFORE BUILDING
#  ------------------------------------------
#  The build context MUST be the REPOSITORY ROOT, not `harness/`, because this
#  file copies three artifacts that only resolve relative to the repository
#  root: `requirements.txt`, `presql2-latest.zip` and
#  `mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip`. `harness/docker-compose.yml` declares
#  `context: ..` with `dockerfile: harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol` precisely so
#  those copies resolve, and names the service `gnucobol`.
#
#      Preferred:  docker compose -f harness/docker-compose.yml build gnucobol
#      Equivalent: docker build -f harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol \
#                      -t acas-oracle-gnucobol .      # <-- run from repo root
#
#  Building with `harness/` as the context FAILS at the first COPY, by design.
#  That failure is a feature: the contract enforces itself.
#
#  THE COMPILE RECIPE IS FROZEN -- R-4, AND THE MOST IMPORTANT RULE HERE
#  --------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The compiler must be invoked with DEFAULT ARITHMETIC, and this image must
#  not alter that. A census of every `cobc` invocation reachable by the oracle
#  build finds NO `-std=` dialect selection, NO `>>SET ARITHMETIC` directive in
#  any source, and NO `binary-truncate` flag anywhere. The flags the frozen
#  scripts actually use are exactly:
#      -m  -x  -Wlinkage  -I ../copybooks  cobmysqlapi.o
#      -L/usr/local/mysql/lib   [common/comp-common.sh:L26]
#      -L /usr/local/mysql/lib  (space variant) [common/comp-common.sh:L32,L36]
#      -lmysqlclient  -lz  -T <name>.prn        [common/comp-common.sh:L26,L51]
#      -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=1  -fdump=ws  -fmissing-statement=ok
#                                               [general/comp-gl.sh:L2-L5]
#      -lncursesw -A '-DHAVE_NCURSESW_NCURSES_H -DDECIMAL_RIGHT'
#                                               [common/comp-common.sh:L18]
#  Nothing in this image adds to, removes from or re-spells that list. In
#  particular it does not enable `-g -d`, `-fdump=ALL` or `-ftraceall`, which
#  appear only in the `*-diags*.sh` script variants the harness never runs.
#
#  TWO STALE-RECORD DEFECTS THAT ARE DELIBERATELY *NOT* FIXED
#  ----------------------------------------------------------
#  A defect reproduced is correct; a defect fixed is a failure. Both of the
#  following are inconsistencies between the maintainer's changelog comments
#  and his live code. Both are left exactly as they are:
#
#    1. [common/comp-common.sh:L8-L9] records that `-Wno-goto-section` was
#       "added to remove the silly default warning in latest gnucobol v3.2",
#       and [README.TXT:L204-L212] says the same. It was NOT added. A repo-wide
#       search finds `-Wno-goto-section` in exactly three places, all of them
#       changelog comments -- [common/comp-common.sh:L8],
#       [common/comp-common2.sh:L8] and
#       [common/comp-common-no-rdbms-diags.sh:L9] -- and in ZERO live `cobc`
#       invocations. This image does not add it. Adding a flag alters the
#       recipe, and the inter-section `GO TO` warnings it would suppress are
#       diagnostics only: they change no computed value and no table state.
#
#    2. [common/comp-common.sh:L11] claims "Added to all comps -fdump=all", yet
#       no live line in that file uses `-fdump` at all. (The General Ledger
#       script does use `-fdump=ws` -- a different thing -- at
#       [general/comp-gl.sh:L2-L5].) Left as found.
#
#  Two further asymmetries are noted here so that no one "harmonises" them:
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L59] and [comp-all.sh:L44-L45] both end with an
#  unconditional `exit 0`, so NEITHER script's exit status is a build-success
#  signal -- `build_oracle.sh` must scan captured output instead, which is why
#  this image neither wraps, colourises, filters nor reorders their output; and
#  `sales/comp-sales.sh` alone omits `-fdump=ws`/`-fmissing-statement=ok` and
#  has its `accept_numeric` lines commented out. All left as found.
#
#  THE CHECKOUT IS MOUNTED READ-ONLY -- NOTHING HERE WRITES TO IT
#  --------------------------------------------------------------
#  The frozen artifacts are read in place from `$ACAS_REPO` (/repo), mounted
#  READ-ONLY, and `build_oracle.sh` copies them to the writable `$ACAS_BUILD`
#  (/build) before compiling. This is not fastidiousness; running the frozen
#  scripts in the checkout would damage it five separate ways:
#    1. [common/comp-common.sh:L25] runs `presql2` over every `*MT.scb`, which
#       OVERWRITES the committed, frozen generated bridges `common/*MT.cbl`;
#    2. every compile passes `-T <name>.prn`, creating listing files;
#    3. `cobmysqlapi.o` must sit in the CWD of every compile, so six copies
#       would land in six frozen directories;
#    4. `presql2.param` would appear as a new file in the CWD;
#    5. `cobc -m` / `-x` outputs land in the source directories.
#  Any diff touching the frozen tree is a defect in the migration regardless of
#  how harmless it looks. Accordingly: this image contains no COPY and no RUN
#  that writes anywhere under /repo, and its WORKDIR is /build, never /repo.
#  It also embeds no copy of any `.cbl`, `.scb`, `.cob` or `.sql` file.
#
#  DIVISION OF LABOUR WITH build_oracle.sh -- FIXED, AND STATED IDENTICALLY
#  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The oracle bootstrap has five steps. Steps 1-3 are performed HERE, at image
#  build time, and their artifacts are published at documented paths:
#      1. unpack the vendored `presql2-latest.zip`  -> /opt/presql2-package
#      2. compile the bridge's C interface object   -> /usr/local/lib/acas/cobmysqlapi.o
#      3. build and install the translator          -> /usr/local/bin/presql2
#  Steps 4-5 belong to `harness/build_oracle.sh`, at run time, because they
#  need a live database and a writable copy of the checkout:
#      4. run `common/comp-common.sh` to translate and compile the bridges,
#         handlers and loaders
#      5. run `comp-all.sh`, which compiles the application directories in the
#         maintainer's own order: common, general, irs, purchase, sales, stock
#         [comp-all.sh:L15-L32]  (OE/payroll/epos are commented out there)
#  `build_oracle.sh` re-runs steps 1-3 only if these artifacts are absent, and
#  copies `cobmysqlapi.o` into each of those six directories because every
#  frozen compile line links a BARE `cobmysqlapi.o` from the current directory.
#
#  DETERMINISM
#  -----------
#  `tests/determinism/test_two_runs_byte_identical.py` requires two runs of the
#  same scenario under the same pinned clock to produce byte-identical dumps.
#  Nothing here bakes in a wall-clock timestamp, a build date, a hostname, a
#  VCS revision or a run identifier, and every version is pinned -- including
#  the GnuCOBOL tarball, by SHA-256 -- so the oracle's verdict cannot drift
#  between builds.
#
#  NO CREDENTIALS
#  --------------
#  No credential literal appears anywhere in this file, including in comments.
#  `ACAS_DB_USER` and `ACAS_DB_PASSWORD` are deliberately NOT declared as ENV at
#  all -- not even empty. BuildKit's `SecretsUsedInArgOrEnv` lint fires on a
#  credential-shaped ENV name regardless of its value, and it is right to: an
#  ENV entry is permanent image metadata that `docker inspect` exposes. The two
#  names are documented instead, here and at the ENV block, so the contract
#  stays greppable without entering a layer. `harness/docker-compose.yml`
#  supplies both at run time, exactly as the sibling `harness/Dockerfile.mariadb`
#  does for its own `MARIADB_*` set.
#  The vendored `presql2-package/presql2.param` DOES carry example credential
#  literals; this image neither copies it to a working directory nor reproduces
#  its values -- only its six-card SHAPE is documented, in placeholder form.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  BASE IMAGE
#
#  Pinned to the Ubuntu LTS release the plan records as the reference
#  environment: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, whose system CPython is 3.12.3 with a
#  `decimal` module backed by the C libmpdec implementation rather than the
#  pure-Python fallback. That is not a convenience -- every monetary and
#  quantity operation in the migrated cycle passes through `decimal.Decimal`.
#
#  The tag is a concrete release, never `latest` and never `rolling`: an
#  arbiter that changes underneath the comparison is not an arbiter.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM ubuntu:24.04

LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="ACAS posting-cycle oracle - GnuCOBOL" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="GnuCOBOL 3.2 final, MySQL client libraries under /usr/local/mysql, the JC preSQL translator and CPython 3.12, for compiling and driving the compiled-COBOL oracle of the ACAS posting-cycle migration." \
      org.opencontainers.image.licenses="GPL-3.0-or-later" \
      acas.harness.role="oracle-builder-and-runner" \
      acas.harness.cobc="3.2" \
      acas.harness.mysql.prefix="/usr/local/mysql" \
      acas.harness.python="3.12"


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  BUILD-TIME PINS
#
#  Declared as ARGs so a reviewer can see every external input in one place and
#  a maintainer can audit or mirror them. All are exact; none is a range, a
#  branch or a "latest".
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

#  GnuCOBOL 3.2 FINAL. The version is repository evidence, not a preference:
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L8-L9]  "... in latest gnucobol v3.2."
#  [README.TXT:L53]  "... migrated over to using the GnuCobol compiler
#                     (3.2 final) now some years back."
#  [README.TXT:L168] "For development and testing I am now using v3.2 final
#                     instead of playing with 3.3 A/Beta etc ..."
#  Distribution packages are commonly 3.1.x or 4.x and are therefore NOT used;
#  the release tarball is fetched from the GNU mirror and verified by digest.
#  The digest below was confirmed byte-identical between ftp.gnu.org and the
#  SourceForge release download, and the tree self-reports
#  PACKAGE_STRING='GnuCOBOL 3.2' with NEWS headline "GnuCOBOL 3.2 (20230728)"
#  -- i.e. FINAL, not 3.2rc1/rc2.
ARG GNUCOBOL_VERSION=3.2
ARG GNUCOBOL_TARBALL=gnucobol-3.2.tar.xz
ARG GNUCOBOL_URL=https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gnucobol/gnucobol-3.2.tar.xz
ARG GNUCOBOL_SHA256=3bb48af46ced4779facf41fdc2ee60e4ccb86eaa99d010b36685315df39c2ee2

#  MariaDB Connector/C, vendored in the checkout as
#  `mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip`. See the CLIENT LIBRARY layer below for
#  why this is the connector used and MySQL Connector/C 6.1.11 is not.
ARG MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_VERSION=3.3.4
ARG MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP=mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip

#  JC preSQL, vendored as `presql2-latest.zip` (61 entries). TWO version
#  strings coexist in that package and BOTH are correct facts about it:
#  [presql2-package/README.SVN:L22] "presql release (currently 1.14f)" and
#  [presql2-package/presql2.cbl:L302]
#      `03  ws-Prog-Version  pic x(6)  value " 2.22 ".`
#  Recorded, not resolved -- reconciling them would be an invention.
ARG PRESQL2_ZIP=presql2-latest.zip
ARG PRESQL2_PACKAGE_VERSION=1.14f
ARG PRESQL2_PROGRAM_VERSION=2.22

#  Where the frozen scripts require the client library and headers to be. Not
#  a choice: [common/comp-common.sh:L26] hard-codes `-L/usr/local/mysql/lib`
#  and [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh] hard-codes
#  `-I/usr/local/mysql/include`.
ARG MYSQL_PREFIX=/usr/local/mysql

#  Where GnuCOBOL is installed. Also not a free choice: lines 1 and 2 of the
#  repository's own loader configuration are `/usr/local/lib/gnucobol` and
#  `/usr/local/lib` [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf], which are exactly where
#  a source build with `--prefix=/usr/local` places libcob and the default
#  module search directory. The frozen config is independent evidence that a
#  source build into /usr/local is what this system expects.
ARG GNUCOBOL_PREFIX=/usr/local

#  Canonical publication path for the bridge's C interface object (step 2).
ARG ACAS_LIB_DIR=/usr/local/lib/acas


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  NON-INTERACTIVE, PREDICTABLE ENVIRONMENT
#
#  `DEBIAN_FRONTEND` is an ARG rather than an ENV on purpose: it must suppress
#  apt prompting during the BUILD without persisting into the running
#  container, where it would degrade any interactive `apt` a maintainer runs
#  while investigating an oracle result.
#
#  A UTF-8 locale is set because the compiled programs use curses screen
#  sections and because the frozen schema's character-set behaviour must not
#  vary with the ambient locale. C.UTF-8 is built into glibc, so it needs no
#  locale generation and adds no variability.
#
#  `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` keeps the harness Python tools from scattering
#  `__pycache__` directories into a bind-mounted working copy, which also keeps
#  the working tree clean; `PYTHONUNBUFFERED` makes their output interleave
#  faithfully with the COBOL scripts' output in a captured log.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARG DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

ENV LANG=C.UTF-8 \
    LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 \
    PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  SYSTEM PACKAGES
#
#  Grouped by the reason each is required. Versions are not pinned per package:
#  the `ubuntu:24.04` tag pins the archive snapshot, and per-package pinning
#  against a moving security pocket makes the build fail rather than drift --
#  the wrong trade for an arbiter that must build reliably. The versions
#  actually resolved are printed into the build log below, so a reviewer can
#  see exactly what was installed.
#
#  BUILD TOOLCHAIN -- GnuCOBOL and the connector are both source builds, and
#  the recovered C-interface rule [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh] invokes
#  `gcc` directly:
#      build-essential gcc make cmake autoconf automake libtool bison flex m4
#      pkg-config
#
#  GnuCOBOL DEPENDENCIES:
#      libgmp-dev      mandatory; the multiple-precision maths library
#      libdb-dev       Berkeley DB, for INDEXED (ISAM) file I/O. CRITICAL --
#                      see the GnuCOBOL layer; without it the compiled menus
#                      divert into an interactive program and the harness hangs
#      libncurses-dev  the WIDE-character ncurses headers. Required because
#                      [common/comp-common.sh:L18] compiles
#                        cobc -m accept_numeric.c -lncursesw \
#                             -A '-DHAVE_NCURSESW_NCURSES_H -DDECIMAL_RIGHT'
#                      NOTE: `libncursesw5-dev` and `libncurses5-dev` DO NOT
#                      EXIST on Ubuntu 24.04; `libncurses-dev` is the package
#                      that now ships ncursesw/ncurses.h. Verified by
#                      apt-cache policy on this base image
#      ncurses-term    the terminfo database, so a real terminal type exists at
#                      run time for the curses screen sections
#      libxml2-dev     XML GENERATE support (GnuCOBOL's default XML handler)
#      libcjson-dev    JSON GENERATE support
#
#  LINK-TIME LIBRARIES the frozen recipe names:
#      zlib1g-dev      every RDBMS-touching compile passes `-lz`
#                      [common/comp-common.sh:L26]
#      libssl-dev      required to build MariaDB Connector/C with TLS support
#
#  ARCHIVE HANDLING -- the vendored archives are unpacked INSIDE the container
#  and never modified in the checkout. `unzip` is not present in the base
#  image, so it is installed explicitly rather than assumed:
#      unzip xz-utils tar ca-certificates curl wget
#
#  RUN-TIME REQUIREMENTS of the harness scripts:
#      util-linux      provides `script`, so `run_cobol_scenario.sh` can
#                      allocate a pty for the curses menus
#      mariadb-client  `mariadb` / `mariadb-admin`, used by `reset_db.sh` and
#                      `seed.sh` to apply the frozen schema and probe readiness
#      python3         CPython 3.12.3 on this base image
#      python3-pip     installs the exactly pinned dependency set
#      python3-venv    the standard-library venv module, kept available for
#                      ad-hoc isolation without changing the default python3
#      locales         a UTF-8 locale for predictable curses behaviour
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hadolint ignore=DL3008
RUN set -eu; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        autoconf \
        automake \
        bison \
        build-essential \
        ca-certificates \
        cmake \
        curl \
        flex \
        gcc \
        libcjson-dev \
        libdb-dev \
        libgmp-dev \
        libncurses-dev \
        libssl-dev \
        libtool \
        libxml2-dev \
        locales \
        m4 \
        make \
        mariadb-client \
        ncurses-term \
        pkg-config \
        python3 \
        python3-pip \
        python3-venv \
        tar \
        unzip \
        util-linux \
        wget \
        xz-utils \
        zlib1g-dev; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    \
    echo '--- resolved toolchain versions (build-log evidence) ---'; \
    gcc --version; \
    cmake --version; \
    python3 --version; \
    mariadb --version; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the wide-character ncurses headers are present ---'; \
    test -f /usr/include/ncursesw/ncurses.h \
      || { echo 'FATAL: ncursesw/ncurses.h is absent; [common/comp-common.sh:L18] compiles accept_numeric.c with -DHAVE_NCURSESW_NCURSES_H and would fail.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the Berkeley DB header is present (ISAM backend) ---'; \
    test -f /usr/include/db.h \
      || { echo 'FATAL: db.h is absent; GnuCOBOL would configure with the indexed file handler DISABLED and the compiled menus would divert into sys002 and hang.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo 'OK: system packages installed; ncursesw and Berkeley DB headers verified.'


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  THE MySQL CLIENT LIBRARY UNDER /usr/local/mysql -- MANDATORY, NOT OPTIONAL
#
#  Every RDBMS-touching compile in the frozen scripts hard-codes this prefix,
#  and the scripts may not be edited, so the FILESYSTEM is made to match the
#  SCRIPTS rather than the other way round:
#
#    [common/comp-common.sh:L26]  cobc -m $i cobmysqlapi.o -I ../copybooks \
#                                  -Wlinkage -L/usr/local/mysql/lib \
#                                  -lmysqlclient -lz -T $i.prn
#    [common/comp-common.sh:L32]  ... -L /usr/local/mysql/lib ...   (space)
#    [common/comp-common.sh:L34]  ... -L/usr/local/mysql/lib  ...
#    [common/comp-common.sh:L51]  cobc -x $i cobmysqlapi.o ... (the *LD loaders)
#    [general/comp-gl.sh:L2-L5]   ... -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient -lz
#    [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh]
#                                 gcc -I/usr/local/mysql/include \
#                                     -c cobmysqlapi38.c -o cobmysqlapi.o -fPIC
#
#  So this layer MUST deliver, at those exact paths:
#      /usr/local/mysql/lib/libmysqlclient.so   (for `-lmysqlclient`)
#      /usr/local/mysql/include/mysql.h         (cobmysqlapi38.c does
#                                                `#include <mysql.h>`)
#  Note the two spellings of `-L` above -- with and without a space. Both work
#  unchanged; neither script is "normalised".
#
#  WHICH CONNECTOR, AND WHY -- MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
#  -------------------------------------------------
#  Two vendored candidates exist and the exact-name match was tried FIRST.
#
#  REJECTED: MySQL Connector/C 6.1.11 (`mysql-connector-c-6.1.11-src.tar.gz`).
#  This is the literal `-lmysqlclient` provider and would have been preferred.
#  It was built in this exact base image and FAILS AT CMAKE CONFIGURE, before
#  any compilation:
#      -- LibMySQL 6.1.11
#      -- Built from MySQL 5.7.16 sources
#      CMake Error at cmake/install_macros.cmake:365 (ENDIF):
#        Flow control statements are not properly nested.
#      -- Configuring incomplete, errors occurred!
#  The 2017-era CMake scripts are rejected by CMake 3.28 on Ubuntu 24.04.
#  Making it build would mean PATCHING the vendored CMake scripts -- risk taken
#  on the client library for no behavioural gain. The archive is therefore not
#  even copied into the image; recording the failure here is more useful than
#  carrying 3.4 MB of unusable source in a layer.
#
#  USED: MariaDB Connector/C 3.3.4 (`mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip`). It
#  builds cleanly here and is well matched to the server, which is MariaDB
#  10.11.7 per the frozen schema dump header [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L1,L5].
#
#  This substitution is an accommodation of the BUILD HOST, never of the
#  recipe: the compiler, the client libraries and the vendored translator
#  remain exactly what the repository specifies, and no frozen script, flag or
#  path is changed. `-lmysqlclient` and `-I/usr/local/mysql/include` continue
#  to resolve verbatim [common/comp-common.sh:L26].
#
#  HOW THE MySQL-STYLE LAYOUT IS OBTAINED -- NO SYMLINK SHIM NEEDED
#  ---------------------------------------------------------------
#  MariaDB Connector/C's DEFAULT layout would install to `lib/mariadb` and
#  `include/mariadb`, which the frozen `-L.../lib` and `-I.../include` would
#  not find. Rather than paper over that with symlinks, the layout is set at
#  configure time -- `cmake/install.cmake:L112-L135` of the vendored source
#  makes INSTALL_LIBDIR, INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR, INSTALL_PLUGINDIR and
#  INSTALL_PCDIR overridable cache variables:
#      -DINSTALL_LIBDIR=lib  -DINSTALL_INCLUDEDIR=include
#      -DINSTALL_PLUGINDIR=lib/plugin
#  giving the flat, MySQL-style tree the frozen recipe expects, directly.
#
#      -DWITH_MYSQLCOMPAT=ON emits the compatibility names the frozen
#      `-lmysqlclient` requires: libmysqlclient.so -> libmariadb.so.3,
#      libmysqlclient.a -> libmariadbclient.a, plus the `_r` variants.
#      -DWITH_EXTERNAL_ZLIB=ON uses the system zlib, matching the `-lz` the
#      frozen recipe links [common/comp-common.sh:L26].
#      -DWITH_UNIT_TESTS=OFF -- the connector's own test suite needs a live
#      server and contributes nothing to the oracle.
#
#  The runtime SONAME remains `libmariadb.so.3`, which the loader resolves
#  because `/usr/local/mysql/lib` is line 3 of the repository's own loader
#  configuration [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf], reproduced verbatim below.
#
#  `make -j` here parallelises a THIRD-PARTY SOURCE BUILD only. It touches no
#  COBOL and cannot reach the compile recipe, so it does not engage the
#  no-concurrency rule (R-3), which governs scenario execution against the
#  shared database.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
COPY ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP} /tmp/vendored/

# hadolint ignore=DL3003
RUN set -eu; \
    cd /tmp/vendored; \
    unzip -q "${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP}"; \
    src="mariadb-connector-c-${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_VERSION}-src"; \
    test -d "${src}" \
      || { echo "FATAL: expected ${src}/ inside ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    mkdir -p "${src}/build-acas"; \
    cd "${src}/build-acas"; \
    cmake .. \
        -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="${MYSQL_PREFIX}" \
        -DINSTALL_LIBDIR=lib \
        -DINSTALL_INCLUDEDIR=include \
        -DINSTALL_PLUGINDIR=lib/plugin \
        -DINSTALL_PCDIR=lib/pkgconfig \
        -DWITH_MYSQLCOMPAT=ON \
        -DWITH_EXTERNAL_ZLIB=ON \
        -DWITH_UNIT_TESTS=OFF; \
    make -j"$(nproc)"; \
    make install; \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf /tmp/vendored; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the frozen recipe resolves: -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient ---'; \
    test -e "${MYSQL_PREFIX}/lib/libmysqlclient.so" \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${MYSQL_PREFIX}/lib/libmysqlclient.so is absent; every frozen compile line linking -lmysqlclient would fail. [common/comp-common.sh:L26]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    echo '--- asserting the recovered C-interface rule resolves: -I/usr/local/mysql/include ---'; \
    test -f "${MYSQL_PREFIX}/include/mysql.h" \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${MYSQL_PREFIX}/include/mysql.h is absent; cobmysqlapi38.c does #include <mysql.h> and would fail. [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- installed client library (build-log evidence) ---'; \
    ls -l "${MYSQL_PREFIX}/lib"; \
    "${MYSQL_PREFIX}/bin/mariadb_config" --version; \
    echo 'OK: MySQL-compatible client library and headers in place under the frozen prefix.'



# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  GnuCOBOL 3.2 FINAL -- BUILT FROM THE RELEASE TARBALL, NOT FROM APT
#
#  A distribution package is not acceptable here unless it can be PROVEN to be
#  3.2 final, and distribution packages are commonly 3.1.x or 4.x. The release
#  tarball is fetched from the GNU mirror and verified against the SHA-256 in
#  the ARG block, so the arbiter's compiler is bit-reproducible.
#
#  `--prefix=/usr/local` is corroborated by the repository itself: lines 1-2 of
#  [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf] are `/usr/local/lib/gnucobol` and
#  `/usr/local/lib`, which is precisely where a `--prefix=/usr/local` build
#  places the default module search directory and libcob.
#
#  ############################################################################
#  ##  CRITICAL: THE ISAM BACKEND. WITHOUT IT THE ORACLE HANGS.              ##
#  ############################################################################
#  The menus force COBOL INDEXED-FILE processing for the system parameter file
#  regardless of whether the RDBMS is in use. [general/general.cbl:L385-L396]:
#
#      Open-System.
#          move     zeros to File-System-Used
#                            File-Duplicates-In-Use.
#          move     "00" to  FA-RDBMS-Flat-Statuses.  *> Force Cobol proc.
#          move     1 to File-Key-No.
#          perform  System-Open-Input.
#          if       fs-reply not = zero
#                   move    "sys002" to ws-called
#                   call    ws-called using ws-calling-data file-defs
#                   perform System-Open
#          end-if.
#
#  and the same pattern repeats at [general/general.cbl:L398-L417] for keys
#  4, 2 and 1. If that indexed OPEN INPUT fails, control passes to `sys002`,
#  which asks a human for the parameters -- so in a harness it blocks forever.
#  A GnuCOBOL configured WITHOUT an indexed handler makes that failure certain.
#
#  Hence `--with-db`, documented by the tarball's own configure as
#  "(GnuCOBOL) Use Berkeley DB >= 4.1 for ISAM I/O", and hence the assertion
#  below that `cobc --info` reports a REAL handler rather than `disabled`.
#  GnuCOBOL prints that line from `cobc/cobc.c:L2724-L2740`, where `WITH_DB`
#  yields "BDB" and the fallback yields "disabled".
#
#  A SECOND interactive trap, noted here because avoiding it is the runner's
#  and the scenarios' job rather than this image's:
#  [general/general.cbl:L462-L463] `if scycle = zero  go to call-system-setup.`
#  -- a zero accounting cycle also diverts into interactive setup, so every
#  scenario must seed a non-zero cycle.
#
#  Other configure choices, each with its reason:
#      --with-curses=ncursesw  the WIDE-character curses library, matching
#                              [common/comp-common.sh:L18], which compiles
#                              accept_numeric.c with -lncursesw and
#                              -DHAVE_NCURSESW_NCURSES_H
#      --with-math=gmp         GMP is mandatory for GnuCOBOL's decimal
#                              arithmetic. This is the compiler's OWN
#                              arithmetic support library; it is not a
#                              directive and does not alter the DEFAULT
#                              arithmetic the frozen recipe relies on (R-4)
#      --with-json=cjson       JSON GENERATE support
#      --with-xml2             XML GENERATE support (GnuCOBOL's default)
#      --with-dl               the system dynamic loader, which is how
#                              `cobc -m` modules are resolved at run time
#
#  Deliberately ABSENT: any `--enable-debug`, `--enable-cobc-internal-checks`,
#  `--enable-debuglog` or `--enable-experimental`. The arbiter must be the
#  maintainer's compiler, not a more talkative one.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hadolint ignore=DL3003
RUN set -eu; \
    mkdir -p /tmp/gnucobol; \
    cd /tmp/gnucobol; \
    curl -fsSL -o "${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL}" "${GNUCOBOL_URL}"; \
    echo "${GNUCOBOL_SHA256}  ${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL}" > SHA256SUMS; \
    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL} failed its SHA-256 check; refusing to build an unverified arbiter." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    tar -xJf "${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL}"; \
    cd "gnucobol-${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}"; \
    grep -qx "PACKAGE_STRING='GnuCOBOL ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}'" configure \
      || { echo "FATAL: the tarball does not self-report GnuCOBOL ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}; a release candidate or a different release was fetched." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    ./configure \
        --prefix="${GNUCOBOL_PREFIX}" \
        --with-db \
        --with-curses=ncursesw \
        --with-math=gmp \
        --with-json=cjson \
        --with-xml2 \
        --with-dl; \
    make -j"$(nproc)"; \
    make install; \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf /tmp/gnucobol; \
    ldconfig; \
    \
    echo '--- cobc --version (build-log evidence; MUST be 3.2) ---'; \
    cobc --version > /tmp/cobc-version.txt; \
    cat /tmp/cobc-version.txt; \
    head -n 1 /tmp/cobc-version.txt > /tmp/cobc-version-line.txt; \
    grep -Eq "^cobc \\(GnuCOBOL\\) ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}(\\.[0-9]+)*\$" /tmp/cobc-version-line.txt \
      || { echo "FATAL: cobc does not report GnuCOBOL ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}. The oracle MUST be the compiler the maintainer targets. [common/comp-common.sh:L8-L9] [README.TXT:L53] [README.TXT:L168]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- cobc --info (build-log evidence) ---'; \
    cobc --info > /tmp/cobc-info.txt 2>&1; \
    cat /tmp/cobc-info.txt; \
    grep -E 'indexed file handler' /tmp/cobc-info.txt > /tmp/cobc-isam.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: cobc --info reports no indexed file handler line at all.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    grep -q 'BDB' /tmp/cobc-isam.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the indexed file handler is not BDB. A GnuCOBOL without ISAM makes the forced indexed OPEN at [general/general.cbl:L385-L396] fail, which CALLs the interactive sys002 and hangs the harness.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    grep -q 'GMP' /tmp/cobc-info.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: cobc --info does not report GMP as the mathematical library; GnuCOBOL decimal arithmetic support is missing.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    rm -f /tmp/cobc-version-line.txt /tmp/cobc-isam.txt; \
    echo "OK: GnuCOBOL ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION} final installed under ${GNUCOBOL_PREFIX} with the BDB indexed file handler."


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  RUNTIME LOADER PATHS -- THE REPOSITORY'S OWN CONFIGURATION, VERBATIM
#
#  [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf] is the SOLE file under `etc/` in the
#  checkout. It is exactly four lines, and they are reproduced here in the SAME
#  ORDER, because search order is part of the configuration:
#
#      /usr/local/lib/gnucobol      <- GnuCOBOL's default module directory
#      /usr/local/lib               <- libcob
#      /usr/local/mysql/lib         <- the client library (libmariadb.so.3)
#      /usr/lib                     <- the distribution's own libraries
#
#  The repository's copy is READ and left UNTOUCHED -- the plan is explicit
#  that this file "is read by the harness image build and left untouched". It
#  is therefore transcribed here rather than COPYed, so that no build step can
#  ever write back to it, and so that this layer works even when /repo is not
#  mounted. The assertion below proves the transcription is exact, four lines
#  and in order.
#
#  `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` repeats the two /usr/local entries as belt and braces
#  alongside ldconfig, which matters when a harness script runs a freshly
#  linked module before any further ldconfig.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eu; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '/usr/local/lib/gnucobol' \
        '/usr/local/lib' \
        '/usr/local/mysql/lib' \
        '/usr/lib' \
        > /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf; \
    ldconfig; \
    \
    echo '--- /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf (must match [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf] exactly) ---'; \
    cat -A /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf; \
    n_lines="$(wc -l < /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf)"; \
    [ "${n_lines}" = '4' ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: expected exactly 4 loader path lines, found ${n_lines}. [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    n=0; \
    for expected in /usr/local/lib/gnucobol /usr/local/lib /usr/local/mysql/lib /usr/lib; do \
        n=$((n + 1)); \
        actual="$(sed -n "${n}p" /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf)"; \
        [ "${actual}" = "${expected}" ] \
          || { echo "FATAL: loader path line ${n} is '${actual}', expected '${expected}'. The search ORDER is part of the configuration. [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting libcob and the client library both resolve ---'; \
    ldconfig -p > /tmp/ldconfig-p.txt; \
    grep -q 'libcob\.so' /tmp/ldconfig-p.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: libcob.so does not resolve through the loader cache.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    grep -q 'libmariadb\.so\.3' /tmp/ldconfig-p.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: libmariadb.so.3 (the SONAME behind libmysqlclient.so) does not resolve through the loader cache.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    rm -f /tmp/ldconfig-p.txt; \
    echo 'OK: loader paths reproduce [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf] exactly, and both libraries resolve.'



# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  ORACLE BOOTSTRAP STEPS 1-3 -- INCLUDING THE BUILD RULE THAT WAS MISSING
#
#  STEP 1: unpack the vendored JC preSQL package.
#  The archive holds 61 entries -- the `presql2-package/` directory entry plus
#  60 members, which is the count the assertion below prints -- and contains
#  `cobmysqlapi38.c`, `cobmysqlapi38.sh`, `presql2.cbl`, `presql2.sh`,
#  `bldcopy2.*`, `prtschema2.*`, `preo.cbl`, `prei.scb`, `test1.cbl`,
#  `presql2.param`, the READMEs and PDFs, plus `ACAS/`, `Variations/` and
#  `old-apis/`. It is unpacked INSIDE the container -- the plan requires the
#  vendored archives to be "unpacked inside the container and never modified in
#  the checkout" -- and published at /opt/presql2-package.
#
#  ##########################################################################
#  ##  STEP 2: THE RECOVERED BUILD RULE FOR cobmysqlapi.o                  ##
#  ##########################################################################
#  Every bridge, handler and loader compile links an object file that provides
#  the C interface between COBOL and the MySQL client library --
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L26], [:L32], [:L34], [:L51] all pass a bare
#  `cobmysqlapi.o`, and so does [general/comp-gl.sh:L2-L5]. That object is
#  ABSENT from the checkout and NO RULE ANYWHERE IN THE REPOSITORY BUILDS IT.
#  Following the maintainer's compile scripts alone, the oracle cannot be built
#  at all: the build dies at link time with no obvious cause.
#
#  The rule was recovered from the vendored translator package and is used here
#  verbatim -- [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh]:
#
#      gcc -I/usr/local/mysql/include -c cobmysqlapi38.c -o cobmysqlapi.o -fPIC
#
#  `cobmysqlapi38.c` does `#include <mysql.h>` and `#include <libcob.h>`, which
#  is why this layer runs AFTER both the client library and GnuCOBOL: mysql.h
#  comes from ${MYSQL_PREFIX}/include and libcob.h from
#  ${GNUCOBOL_PREFIX}/include.
#
#  ONLY `cobmysqlapi38.c` IS USED. The package also ships two SUPERSEDED
#  variants that must NOT be used, and their own build lines show why:
#      [presql2-package/old-apis/cobmysqlapi.sh]
#          gcc -I/usr/include/mysql -c cobmysqlapi.005.c
#      [presql2-package/old-apis/cobmysqlapi3.sh]
#          gcc -I/usr/include/mysql -c cobmysqlapi3.c -o cobmysqlapi.o
#  Both use a DIFFERENT include path and both OMIT `-fPIC` -- which the frozen
#  `cobc -m` (dynamically loadable module) builds require.
#
#  The object is published at ${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o. `build_oracle.sh`
#  copies it into each of the six compile directories, because the frozen
#  compile lines name a BARE `cobmysqlapi.o` resolved from the current working
#  directory -- another reason those compiles cannot run inside the checkout.
#
#  STEP 3: build and install the translator, verbatim from
#  [presql2-package/presql2.sh]:
#
#      cobc -x presql2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib \
#           -lmysqlclient -lz
#
#  The sibling copybook utility is built too, from its own rule, so the
#  toolchain is complete rather than minimal -- [presql2-package/bldcopy2.sh]:
#
#      cobc -x bldcopy2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L /usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient
#
#  (note the SPACE after `-L`, and no `-lz` -- the second of the two `-L`
#  spellings that must both keep working, matching
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L32] and [:L36]).
#
#  Both translator sources take their working storage and procedure text from
#  the package's own copybooks, so all four are staged alongside them:
#      [presql2-package/presql2.cbl:L467]   COPY MYSQL-VARIABLES.
#      [presql2-package/presql2.cbl:L2685]  COPY MYSQL-PROCEDURES.
#      [presql2-package/bldcopy2.cbl:L163]  copy "mysql-variables.cpy".
#      [presql2-package/bldcopy2.cbl:L495]  copy mysql-procedures.
#  The package ships BOTH cases -- `MYSQL-VARIABLES.CPY` / `MYSQL-PROCEDURES.CPY`
#  and `mysql-variables.cpy` / `mysql-procedures.cpy` -- and they are NOT the
#  same size, so they are not duplicates to be de-duplicated. All four are
#  staged and `COBCPY` is pointed at the staging directory for this layer only.
#
#  `prtschema2` is NOT built here, and the reason is a fact about the package
#  rather than a choice: [presql2-package/prtschema2.sh] is
#
#      presql2 prtschema2.scb prtschema2.cbl
#      cobc -x prtschema2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib \
#           -lmysqlclient -lz
#      exit 0
#
#  and `prtschema2.cbl` DOES NOT SHIP -- the package contains only
#  `prtschema2.scb`, `prtschema2.param` and `prtschema2.sh`. The `.cbl` has to
#  be GENERATED by `presql2`, which reads a live `information_schema`, and no
#  database is reachable during an image build. It is also not needed: it is a
#  schema-printing utility, and no frozen compile script invokes it. The
#  already-generated `Variations/prtschema2M.cbl` and
#  `Variations/prtschema2o.cbl` are deliberately NOT substituted -- they are
#  different variants, and swapping one in would be an invention.
#
#  ##########################################################################
#  ##  presql2 REQUIRES A presql2.param FILE IN ITS WORKING DIRECTORY      ##
#  ##########################################################################
#  This is not documented in the compile scripts and will otherwise surprise
#  whoever runs step 4. `read_params()` in
#  [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.c:L114] builds "<progname>.param", fopen()s
#  it, and on absence prints "Could not read file >...<, aborting..." and
#  `exit(5)`. It then requires SIX cards, IN THIS ORDER, each prefix-validated
#  with `exit(6)` on mismatch and each truncated to 32 characters:
#
#      DBHOST=<host>
#      DBUSER=<user>
#      DBPASSWD=<password>
#      DBNAME=<schema>
#      DBPORT=<port>
#      DBSOCKET=<unix socket path, may be empty for TCP>
#
#  NO `.param` file exists anywhere in the checkout, and none is created here:
#  baking one into an image layer would bake in a credential. The placeholders
#  above are the SHAPE only. `harness/build_oracle.sh` materialises the file at
#  run time from ACAS_DB_HOST / ACAS_DB_USER / ACAS_DB_PASSWORD / ACAS_DB_NAME
#  / ACAS_DB_PORT / ACAS_DB_SOCKET, in the working directory where `presql2`
#  runs, OUTSIDE the checkout. Because `presql2` opens a live connection, step 4
#  of the bootstrap additionally requires the `mariadb` service to be healthy.
#  The vendored `presql2-package/presql2.param` is left in place unread as
#  package documentation; it carries example credential literals and must never
#  be copied into a working directory.
#
#  Nothing in this layer is a `cobc` flag change: every command above is
#  transcribed from a vendored `.sh` file (R-4).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
COPY ${PRESQL2_ZIP} /tmp/presql2/

# hadolint ignore=DL3003
RUN set -eu; \
    cd /tmp/presql2; \
    unzip -q "${PRESQL2_ZIP}"; \
    test -d presql2-package \
      || { echo "FATAL: expected presql2-package/ inside ${PRESQL2_ZIP}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    mkdir -p /opt; \
    mv presql2-package /opt/presql2-package; \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf /tmp/presql2; \
    \
    echo "--- JC preSQL package unpacked (package version ${PRESQL2_PACKAGE_VERSION}, program version ${PRESQL2_PROGRAM_VERSION}) ---"; \
    find /opt/presql2-package -mindepth 1 > /tmp/presql2-entries.txt; \
    n_entries="$(wc -l < /tmp/presql2-entries.txt)"; \
    rm -f /tmp/presql2-entries.txt; \
    echo "entries under /opt/presql2-package: ${n_entries}"; \
    for required in cobmysqlapi38.c cobmysqlapi38.sh presql2.cbl presql2.sh \
                    bldcopy2.cbl bldcopy2.sh \
                    MYSQL-VARIABLES.CPY MYSQL-PROCEDURES.CPY \
                    mysql-variables.cpy mysql-procedures.cpy; do \
        test -f "/opt/presql2-package/${required}" \
          || { echo "FATAL: /opt/presql2-package/${required} is missing from the vendored archive." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    test -f /opt/presql2-package/old-apis/cobmysqlapi.005.c \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the vendored archive layout is not as expected; old-apis/ should exist and be left unused.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    test -d /opt/presql2-package/ACAS \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the vendored worked ACAS example is missing; the end-to-end toolchain smoke test depends on it.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    mkdir -p "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}"; \
    build=/tmp/acas-bootstrap; \
    mkdir -p "${build}"; \
    cp -p /opt/presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.c \
          /opt/presql2-package/presql2.cbl \
          /opt/presql2-package/bldcopy2.cbl \
          /opt/presql2-package/MYSQL-VARIABLES.CPY \
          /opt/presql2-package/MYSQL-PROCEDURES.CPY \
          /opt/presql2-package/mysql-variables.cpy \
          /opt/presql2-package/mysql-procedures.cpy \
          "${build}/"; \
    cd "${build}"; \
    COBCPY="${build}"; export COBCPY; \
    COB_COPY_DIR="${build}"; export COB_COPY_DIR; \
    \
    echo '--- STEP 2: the recovered rule, verbatim [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh] ---'; \
    gcc -I/usr/local/mysql/include -c cobmysqlapi38.c -o cobmysqlapi.o -fPIC; \
    install -m 0644 cobmysqlapi.o "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o"; \
    \
    echo '--- STEP 3: the translator, verbatim [presql2-package/presql2.sh] ---'; \
    cobc -x presql2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient -lz; \
    install -m 0755 presql2 /usr/local/bin/presql2; \
    \
    echo '--- STEP 3 (sibling): [presql2-package/bldcopy2.sh] -- note the space after -L ---'; \
    cobc -x bldcopy2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L /usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient; \
    install -m 0755 bldcopy2 /usr/local/bin/bldcopy2; \
    \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf "${build}"; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the published bootstrap artifacts ---'; \
    test -f "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o" \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o was not published; every frozen bridge, handler and loader compile links it. [common/comp-common.sh:L26]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    command -v presql2 > /dev/null \
      || { echo 'FATAL: presql2 is not on the PATH; [common/comp-common.sh:L25] invokes it to translate every *MT.scb.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    command -v bldcopy2 > /dev/null \
      || { echo 'FATAL: bldcopy2 is not on the PATH.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- linkage evidence: presql2 resolves libcob and the client library ---'; \
    ldd /usr/local/bin/presql2 > /tmp/presql2-ldd.txt; \
    cat /tmp/presql2-ldd.txt; \
    if grep -q 'not found' /tmp/presql2-ldd.txt; then \
        echo 'FATAL: presql2 has unresolved shared-library dependencies.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    rm -f /tmp/presql2-ldd.txt; \
    echo "OK: bootstrap steps 1-3 complete -- /opt/presql2-package, ${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o, /usr/local/bin/{presql2,bldcopy2}."


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  END-TO-END TOOLCHAIN PROOF -- THE VENDORED WORKED ACAS EXAMPLE
#
#  This proves the WHOLE chain -- GnuCOBOL 3.2, the client library under the
#  frozen prefix, and the recovered `cobmysqlapi.o` -- before the frozen tree is
#  ever touched, and it does so by compiling a real generated ACAS bridge with
#  the maintainer's own command line. [presql2-package/ACAS/comp-stockMT.sh]:
#
#      #presql2 stockMT.SCB stockMT.cbl        <- commented out in the package
#      cobc -m stockMT.COB cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib \
#           -lmysqlclient -lz
#
#  Reproduced verbatim. A link error here is exactly the failure mode that the
#  absent `cobmysqlapi.o` produces in the real build, where it appears with no
#  obvious cause -- so a clean pass is high-value evidence and is worth a build
#  layer of its own.
#
#  Two properties of this check matter:
#    * it needs NO database. `cobc -m` compiles and links; it does not connect.
#      (`presql2` would connect, which is why its line is commented out in the
#      vendored script and is not run here either.)
#    * it uses the VENDORED example, never the checkout. The frozen
#      `common/*MT.cbl` are not compiled here, /repo is not read, and no
#      artifact of this test survives the layer -- the scratch directory is
#      removed, so nothing is baked into the image and nothing can perturb a
#      later table dump.
#
#  An additional guard proves the ISAM backend WORKS rather than merely being
#  configured, by compiling and running a throwaway program that creates,
#  writes and reads an INDEXED file. This is the capability the forced indexed
#  OPEN at [general/general.cbl:L385-L396] depends on; if it were absent the
#  menus would CALL the interactive `sys002` and the harness would hang.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hadolint ignore=DL3003
RUN set -eu; \
    smoke=/tmp/acas-smoke; \
    rm -rf "${smoke}"; \
    cp -a /opt/presql2-package/ACAS "${smoke}"; \
    cd "${smoke}"; \
    cp -p "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o" ./cobmysqlapi.o; \
    \
    echo '--- reproducing [presql2-package/ACAS/comp-stockMT.sh] verbatim ---'; \
    cobc -m stockMT.COB cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient -lz; \
    test -f stockMT.so \
      || { echo 'FATAL: stockMT.so was not produced. The COBOL-to-MySQL toolchain is incomplete; every frozen bridge compile would fail the same way. [presql2-package/ACAS/comp-stockMT.sh]' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    ldd stockMT.so > ./stockMT.ldd; \
    cat ./stockMT.ldd; \
    if grep -q 'not found' ./stockMT.ldd; then \
        echo 'FATAL: stockMT.so has unresolved shared-library dependencies; -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient did not resolve at run time.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo 'OK: a real generated ACAS bridge compiles and links against the recovered cobmysqlapi.o.'; \
    \
    echo '--- proving the BDB indexed handler actually works, not just that it is configured ---'; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '       identification division.' \
        '       program-id. blitzyisamprobe.' \
        '       environment division.' \
        '       input-output section.' \
        '       file-control.' \
        '           select probe-file assign to "isam-probe"' \
        '               organization indexed' \
        '               access mode dynamic' \
        '               record key probe-key' \
        '               file status probe-status.' \
        '       data division.' \
        '       file section.' \
        '       fd  probe-file.' \
        '       01  probe-rec.' \
        '           03  probe-key   pic x(4).' \
        '           03  probe-data  pic x(8).' \
        '       working-storage section.' \
        '       01  probe-status    pic xx value spaces.' \
        '       procedure division.' \
        '           open output probe-file.' \
        '           if probe-status not = "00"' \
        '               display "ISAM-OPEN-OUTPUT-FAILED " probe-status' \
        '               stop run returning 1' \
        '           end-if.' \
        '           move "K001" to probe-key.' \
        '           move "PAYLOAD1" to probe-data.' \
        '           write probe-rec.' \
        '           if probe-status not = "00"' \
        '               display "ISAM-WRITE-FAILED " probe-status' \
        '               stop run returning 1' \
        '           end-if.' \
        '           close probe-file.' \
        '           open input probe-file.' \
        '           move "K001" to probe-key.' \
        '           read probe-file key is probe-key.' \
        '           if probe-status not = "00" or probe-data not = "PAYLOAD1"' \
        '               display "ISAM-READ-FAILED " probe-status " " probe-data' \
        '               stop run returning 1' \
        '           end-if.' \
        '           close probe-file.' \
        '           display "ISAM-OK".' \
        '           stop run returning 0.' \
        > blitzyisamprobe.cbl; \
    cobc -x blitzyisamprobe.cbl; \
    ./blitzyisamprobe > ./isam.out 2>&1 \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the indexed-file probe failed to run.' >&2; cat ./isam.out; exit 1; }; \
    cat ./isam.out; \
    grep -qx 'ISAM-OK' ./isam.out \
      || { echo 'FATAL: INDEXED file I/O does not work. [general/general.cbl:L385-L396] forces COBOL indexed processing for the system parameter file and, on failure, CALLs the interactive sys002 -- which hangs a harness forever.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf "${smoke}"; \
    test ! -e /tmp/acas-smoke \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the smoke-test scratch directory was not removed; no build artifact may persist into the image.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    echo 'OK: end-to-end toolchain proven, and INDEXED file I/O verified working.'



# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  THE PYTHON SIDE -- EXACTLY THE REPOSITORY'S PINS, AND NOTHING ELSE
#
#  `requirements.txt` is copied from the build context (the repository root)
#  and installed as-is. It carries twelve `name==version` lines and no ranges,
#  no extras, no index URLs, no editable or VCS requirements, and no hashes --
#  so `--require-hashes` is NOT used, because inventing hashes would break
#  installation. The pins are the same ones `pyproject.toml` declares; a
#  mismatch between the two manifests is a defect, so neither is edited here.
#
#  `--break-system-packages` is required because Ubuntu 24.04 marks its system
#  Python PEP-668 externally-managed. Installing into the SYSTEM interpreter is
#  deliberate rather than lazy: it keeps `python3` the Ubuntu-provided CPython
#  3.12.3 that the plan names as the target runtime, with no venv indirection
#  for the harness scripts to activate. The container is single-purpose and
#  disposable, so there is no host environment to protect.
#
#  ##########################################################################
#  ##  R-2 SELF-CHECK: ZERO BINARY FLOATING POINT                          ##
#  ##########################################################################
#  No accounting value may pass through a binary floating-point type at any
#  point -- not in computation, not in storage, not in transport. The exclusion
#  of `pandas` and `numpy` is ABSOLUTE and extends to the harness dump
#  comparison, which uses ordered row sequences rather than dataframes. The
#  assertions below prove BOTH are absent, so a transitive dependency cannot
#  smuggle either in without failing this build.
#
#  The interpreter's `decimal` must be the C libmpdec implementation, not the
#  pure-Python fallback -- every monetary and quantity operation in the
#  migrated cycle passes through it, so this matters for correctness as well as
#  speed. `import _decimal` succeeding is the check for that.
#
#  ALSO DELIBERATELY ABSENT, each for a stated reason:
#      pytest-xdist and every other parallel plugin -- execution is strictly
#          sequential, matching the single-threaded COBOL, and parallel
#          scenario runs against one shared MariaDB would destroy the
#          seed/run/dump/reset/run/dump/diff protocol
#      jsonschema -- not in `requirements.txt`. Everything under `harness/`
#          works with the standard library plus PyYAML plus the driver
#      any ORM entity layer, alembic and every migration tool -- the schema is
#          frozen and no DDL is ever emitted
#      any asyncio / threading / connection-pool helper
#      any third-party date library -- `acas_posting/dates.py` reproduces
#          [common/maps04.cbl] exactly, defects included, and a general-purpose
#          date library would be MORE CORRECT THAN THE SPECIFICATION
#
#  The `acas_posting` package is NOT installed into this image. Nothing here
#  puts `harness/` on `PYTHONPATH` or makes it importable (R-1);
#  `run_python_scenario.sh` runs `python -m acas_posting ...` against the
#  repository working copy, and the harness tools are invoked by path.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
COPY requirements.txt /opt/acas-harness/requirements.txt

RUN set -eu; \
    python3 -m pip install --no-cache-dir --break-system-packages \
        -r /opt/acas-harness/requirements.txt; \
    \
    echo '--- interpreter identity (must be CPython 3.12.x) ---'; \
    python3 --version; \
    python3 -c 'import sys; assert sys.version_info[:2] == (3, 12), f"FATAL: expected CPython 3.12.x, found {sys.version.split()[0]}"'; \
    \
    echo '--- decimal must be the C libmpdec implementation ---'; \
    python3 -c 'import decimal, _decimal; assert decimal.HAVE_THREADS is not None; print("decimal backed by:", _decimal.__name__)'; \
    \
    echo '--- truncation semantics: an un-ROUNDED COBOL store discards, it does not round ---'; \
    python3 -c 'from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_DOWN; q = Decimal("1.239").quantize(Decimal("0.01"), rounding=ROUND_DOWN); assert q == Decimal("1.23"), q; print("ROUND_DOWN 1.239 ->", q)'; \
    \
    echo '--- the pinned set must import ---'; \
    python3 -c 'import mysql.connector, sqlalchemy, yaml, pytest; print("mysql-connector-python", mysql.connector.__version__); print("SQLAlchemy", sqlalchemy.__version__); print("PyYAML", yaml.__version__); print("pytest", pytest.__version__)'; \
    \
    echo '--- R-2 self-check: pandas and numpy MUST be absent ---'; \
    if python3 -c 'import pandas' > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo 'FATAL: pandas is installed. Zero binary floating point in accounting computation -- the exclusion is absolute and extends to the harness dump comparison.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    if python3 -c 'import numpy' > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo 'FATAL: numpy is installed. Zero binary floating point in accounting computation -- the exclusion is absolute and extends to the harness dump comparison.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo 'OK: pandas and numpy are both absent.'; \
    \
    echo '--- no-concurrency self-check: no parallel test plugin may be present ---'; \
    if python3 -c 'import xdist' > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo 'FATAL: pytest-xdist is installed. Execution must be strictly sequential; parallel scenario runs against one shared database would break the seed/run/dump/reset/run/dump/diff protocol.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo 'OK: no parallel test plugin present.'; \
    \
    echo '--- no schema-migration tool may be present ---'; \
    if python3 -c 'import alembic' > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo 'FATAL: alembic is installed. mysql/ACASDB.sql is frozen and no DDL is ever emitted.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo 'OK: no schema-migration tool present.'; \
    \
    echo '--- installed distributions (build-log evidence) ---'; \
    python3 -m pip list --format=freeze; \
    echo 'OK: the Python side is exactly the repository pin set.'



# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  MOUNT POINTS -- THE READ-ONLY / WRITABLE SPLIT THE FROZEN TREE DEPENDS ON
#
#      /repo   the checkout, mounted READ-ONLY. The frozen COBOL, copybooks,
#              bridges and `mysql/ACASDB.sql` are read in place from here.
#      /build  a WRITABLE copy, made by `build_oracle.sh` before it runs the
#              maintainer's compile scripts. Everything the scripts create --
#              regenerated `*MT.cbl`, `.prn` listings, six copies of
#              `cobmysqlapi.o`, `presql2.param`, `.so` modules and executables
#              -- lands here and never in the checkout.
#      /data   the ledger data directory: COBOL indexed files and the flat seed
#              files the loaders read.
#      /out    dumps, normalised dumps and diff evidence.
#
#  /build/bin exists so that ACAS_BIN resolves; see the environment block.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eu; \
    mkdir -p /repo /build /build/bin /data /out; \
    chmod 0755 /repo /build /build/bin /data /out; \
    echo 'OK: /repo (read-only mount point), /build, /build/bin, /data and /out created.'


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  ENVIRONMENT CONTRACT
#
#  DATABASE -- the client-side half of the contract stated in the sibling image
#  `harness/Dockerfile.mariadb`, which serves the schema at compose service
#  name `mariadb` on port 3306. The names must agree across both images and the
#  compose file; the values for user and password come from compose.
#
#  ACAS_DB_USER and ACAS_DB_PASSWORD are deliberately NOT declared here, and
#  the sibling image gives the reason in the same words: defaulting
#  ACAS_DB_PASSWORD to anything would put a credential in an image layer. They
#  are named in this comment so the contract is still greppable and complete;
#  `harness/docker-compose.yml` supplies both at run time, and
#  `build_oracle.sh` reads them from the environment when it materialises
#  `presql2.param`. Declaring even an empty ENV for them is avoided because
#  BuildKit's own `SecretsUsedInArgOrEnv` lint exists precisely to stop a
#  credential-shaped variable being recorded in image metadata -- suppressing
#  that warning rather than heeding it would be the wrong instinct here.
#
#  ACAS_DB_SOCKET *is* declared, and empty, which means "connect over TCP" --
#  the harness reaches the server by hostname, so no unix socket exists to
#  name. An empty socket path is a routing choice, not a secret.
#
#  COBOL RUNTIME
#      COBCPY / COB_COPY_DIR   the copybook search path. [comp-all.sh:L9-L10]
#                              exports these itself as the RELATIVE
#                              `../copybooks`, which is correct while the
#                              scripts are cd'd into a ledger directory. The
#                              absolute default here is compatible with that
#                              and additionally makes ad-hoc `cobc` use work
#                              from any directory; the frozen scripts still win
#                              inside their own run, because they export their
#                              own value.
#      COB_LIBRARY_PATH        where the GnuCOBOL runtime looks for `cobc -m`
#                              modules. Seeded with the SIX compile directories
#                              in the maintainer's own order
#                              [comp-all.sh:L15-L32] -- common, general, irs,
#                              purchase, sales, stock -- followed by GnuCOBOL's
#                              own default module directory, which is line 1 of
#                              [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf].
#                              `build_oracle.sh` and both runners extend it.
#      COB_EXIT_WAIT=off       stops the runtime pausing for a keypress when a
#                              screen program ends. This removes ONLY a
#                              terminal pause: the plan's rule for the
#                              presentation layer is that accept prompts whose
#                              sole effect is to block a terminal are dropped
#                              entirely, while any control transfer around them
#                              is preserved. It changes no computed value and
#                              no table state -- and without it every scenario
#                              run would hang.
#
#      NOT SET: COB_SCREEN_EXCEPTIONS and COB_SCREEN_ESC. The programs set
#      these themselves at [general/general.cbl:L369-L370] (and likewise
#      [general/gl000.cbl:L172-L173]) via `set ENVIRONMENT ... to "Y"`, so
#      presetting them here would be redundant, and leaving them alone keeps
#      the runtime environment demonstrably the programs' own.
#
#  ACAS PATHS -- ACAS_LEDGERS AND ACAS_BIN ARE BOTH LOAD-BEARING
#      A hard, INTERACTIVE gate depends on them.
#      [copybooks/Proc-Get-Env-Set-Files.cob:L20-L28]:
#
#          accept   ACAS_LEDGERS from Environment "ACAS_LEDGERS".
#          accept   ACAS_BIN     from Environment "ACAS_BIN".
#          if       ACAS_LEDGERS (1:1) = spaces
#                or ACAS_BIN (1:1) = spaces
#                   display SY009   at 0505 with erase eos highlight
#                   display SY008   at 1210 with ...
#                   accept ws-reply at 1243
#                   stop run
#          end-if
#
#      If EITHER is blank the program displays a message, waits on an ACCEPT
#      and stops -- the same class of harness hang as the sys002 trap, and it is
#      reached early because [general/general.cbl:L372] performs
#      `zz020-Get-Program-Args`, which performs that section first. Both are
#      therefore set here.
#
#      ACAS_LEDGERS must be an ABSOLUTE path, because
#      [copybooks/Proc-Get-Env-Set-Files.cob:L30-L31] derives the platform
#      delimiter from its first character:
#          if  ACAS_LEDGERS (1:1) = "\" or "/"
#              move ACAS_LEDGERS (1:1) to OS-Delimiter
#      and `zz020-Set-the-Paths` then prefixes every file name with
#      ACAS_LEDGERS + OS-Delimiter, so the value carries NO trailing delimiter.
#
#      ACAS_BIN is where the menus look for a backup script:
#      [general/general.cbl:L489-L503] strings ACAS_BIN, the delimiter and the
#      script name, calls `CBL_CHECK_FILE_EXIST`, and on a non-zero return
#      moves the literal "No BackUp Script in Bin" into a displayed field.
#      A missing script is NON-FATAL -- only the display changes -- but setting
#      the variable and creating the directory makes the behaviour
#      deterministic instead of dependent on the ambient environment.
#
#  TERMINAL -- the compiled menus REFUSE to run on a small terminal.
#  [general/general.cbl:L374-L383]:
#
#      accept   ws-env-lines   from lines.
#      if       ws-env-lines < 24
#               display SY010    at 0101 with erase eos
#               accept  ws-reply at 0133
#               goback.
#      accept   ws-env-Columns from Columns.
#      if       ws-Env-Columns < 80
#               display SY013    at 0101 with erase eos
#               accept  ws-reply at 0133
#               goback.
#
#  So TERM, LINES and COLUMNS are given defaults that satisfy the 24x80 floor.
#  `run_cobol_scenario.sh` still allocates a pty (hence `util-linux` above);
#  these defaults make the container's behaviour predictable rather than
#  dependent on how it was attached.
#
#  BOOTSTRAP ARTIFACT PATHS -- published so `build_oracle.sh` can find and
#  reuse steps 1-3 instead of repeating them.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ENV ACAS_DB_HOST=mariadb \
    ACAS_DB_PORT=3306 \
    ACAS_DB_NAME=ACASDB \
    ACAS_DB_SOCKET="" \
    ACAS_REPO=/repo \
    ACAS_BUILD=/build \
    ACAS_DATA=/data \
    ACAS_OUT=/out \
    ACAS_BIN=/build/bin \
    ACAS_LEDGERS=/data \
    ACAS_PRESQL2_PACKAGE=/opt/presql2-package \
    ACAS_COBMYSQLAPI_OBJ=/usr/local/lib/acas/cobmysqlapi.o \
    ACAS_MYSQL_PREFIX=/usr/local/mysql \
    COBCPY=/build/copybooks \
    COB_COPY_DIR=/build/copybooks \
    COB_LIBRARY_PATH=/build/common:/build/general:/build/irs:/build/purchase:/build/sales:/build/stock:/usr/local/lib/gnucobol \
    COB_EXIT_WAIT=off \
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/local/lib:/usr/local/mysql/lib \
    TERM=xterm \
    LINES=24 \
    COLUMNS=80


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  WORKING DIRECTORY AND DEFAULT COMMAND
#
#  WORKDIR is /build -- the WRITABLE copy -- and deliberately NEVER /repo.
#  Defaulting to the read-only checkout would invite exactly the writes that
#  would damage the frozen tree, and a `cobc` run there would scatter `.prn`
#  listings and `.so` modules through it.
#
#  CMD is a plain shell because compose runs the harness scripts explicitly --
#  `build_oracle.sh`, `seed.sh`, `reset_db.sh`, `run_cobol_scenario.sh`,
#  `run_python_scenario.sh`, then `dump_tables.py`, `normalize.py` and
#  `diff_states.py`. No ENTRYPOINT wrapper is used: a wrapper would sit between
#  the scripts and their output, and because
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L59] and [comp-all.sh:L44-L45] both end with an
#  unconditional `exit 0`, `build_oracle.sh` must scan that output verbatim to
#  detect failure. Nothing here may swallow, colourise, filter or reorder it.
#
#  No supervisor and no process manager is installed: a second concurrent
#  scenario against the shared database would break the parity protocol.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKDIR /build

CMD ["bash"]

